# File spec for ./mod.rs — the lint MODEL and collector core of the ./ linting tree.
# The pipeline order, CLI flags and the exit-1 verdict belong to ./../package/lint.sdd
# (driver — reference shapes, never restate); rule semantics belong to the pending
# sibling specs ./config.sdd, ./hbs_checker.sdd, ./rhai_checker.sdd.
# Grouping note: this spec covers mod.rs only; the three declared submodules get their
# own same-basename specs next, so no multi-file grouping is applied here.

Spec: LintingModel

Platform: Rust

Purpose:
  Own the shared lint vocabulary: the severity ladder @LintLevel, the finding value
    object @LintFinding, the accumulating @LintResultCollector (verdict probe, relative-path
    rebasing, filtered text and JSON report framing, level-less JUnit rollup), the raw-YAML
    line locator @find_section_key_line_numbers and its `options` wrapper
    @find_option_line_numbers, and the module tree that re-exports @LintConfig and
    @parse_inline_disables out of ./config.rs. Every rule code, level assignment and
    finding-producing branch lives at the producer call sites (driver or checkers); this
    file defines only the shapes they fill and the renderings they print.

References:
  ./../package/lint.sdd — the driver and consumer: it calls @new, @add, @extend,
    @has_errors, @prefix_files, @to_text, @to_json and @to_junit exactly once per run path,
    and delegates the report framing (level tag, tally, JSON fields) plus the level-gate
    arithmetic to this file; `--level` values arrive pre-mapped (All → @Info, Warn → @Warn,
    Error → @Error) by the driver's @level_filter_to_lint_level, and the driver's
    `--format` flag selects between `@to_text` and `@to_json`.
  ./config.sdd — sibling spec pending (next to author): @LintConfig::load and
    @resolve_level plus @parse_inline_disables behaviour; this file re-exports those two
    names, and @resolve_level output feeding into built findings is its only consumption
    shape frozen here.
  ./hbs_checker.sdd and ./rhai_checker.sdd — sibling specs pending (next to author): both
    import @LintFinding, @LintLevel, @LintConfig and @parse_inline_disables from
    @crate::linting, return @Vec of findings from @check_file, and the hbs side issues one
    shared @find_option_line_numbers call; their rule codes and levels are owned there.
  ./../package/mod.sdd — the trap around the driver's @Err (exit 1); none of it here.

Depends on:
  ./config.rs — the only submodule this file content-depends on (through the @pub use
    re-export); its spec is ./config.sdd, pending.
  @serde — derives on @LintLevel and @LintFinding.
  @serde_json — the @json macro and @to_string_pretty drive the JSON rendering.
  @junit_report — @ReportBuilder, @TestSuiteBuilder, @TestCase::failure and
    @Duration::seconds as the only used paths of that crate.
  @std — @fmt::Display, @fmt::Write, @path::PathBuf, @path::Path, @fs::read_to_string,
    @str::FromStr, @collections::BTreeMap.
  The @str::parse and @serde deserialization paths of @LintLevel are exercised from the
    ./config.rs YAML load lane, nowhere else in-crate.

Exposes:
  @LintLevel — @pub @Copy enum with exactly three variants in this declaration order:
    @Info, @Warn, @Error; derives @Clone, @Debug, @PartialEq, @Eq, @PartialOrd, @Ord plus
    @Serialize and @Deserialize with NO rename attributes, so the derived @Ord ranks
    @Info < @Warn < @Error (discriminants 0 / 1 / 2 by declaration order) and serde speaks
    the identifiers verbatim in both directions: `"Info"`, `"Warn"`, `"Error"`,
    case-sensitive.
  @Display for @LintLevel — the @Error variant prints `ERROR`, @Warn prints `WARN`,
    @Info prints `INFO` (uppercase, 4–5 chars).
  @FromStr for @LintLevel — @Err type @String; accepts any ASCII-case spelling of the
    literals `error`, `warn`, `info` (whole-string lowercase compare, no trimming, so a
    trailing space fails), otherwise @Err with `Unknown lint level: {s}` carrying the
    caller's original spelling. Production-dead today (Tasks).
  @LintFinding — @pub @Clone and @Debug struct, @Serialize plus @Deserialize; fields in
    declaration order: @rule of @String (the `family/name` code — free-form text as far as
    this file cares, no registry here), @level of @LintLevel, @file of @path::PathBuf
    (real or virtual path as produced), @line of @Option wrapping @usize (`@None`
    tolerated; `Some` values are 1-based by locator convention), @message of @String.
    No @Ord — findings never sort.
  @LintResultCollector — @pub struct with one private @Vec field of findings and NO public
    accessor: raw findings leave only through the renderings below. @new is a @pub @const
    fn; @add and @extend append; plus @has_errors, @prefix_files, @to_text, @to_json,
    @to_junit; the manual @Default impl delegates to @new (same empty state).
  @find_section_key_line_numbers taking @yaml_path and @section — @pub text scanner
    returning @BTreeMap keyed by @String against @usize.
  @find_option_line_numbers taking @yaml_path — @pub thin wrapper calling the scanner with
    the section literal `options`; `options` is its only in-crate argument (Tasks).
  Re-exports at the @crate::linting path: @LintConfig and @parse_inline_disables from
    ./config.rs — both checker import sites bind through these, not through the config path.
  @pub submodule declarations: @config, @hbs_checker, @rhai_checker (own specs pending).

Accepts:
  From @add and @extend: producer-built @LintFinding values — nothing is validated, so
    unknown rule strings, `@None` lines and absolute files all flow through.
  From @to_text and @to_json: one @LintLevel floor built by the caller's mapping; it gates
    display only.
  From @prefix_files: a base @path::Path joined onto the relative finding files.
  From the locators: a path to raw YAML text on disk; the flow form `options: {…}` and the
    block form both open the section, comment and blank traffic is tolerated.
  From @to_junit, @has_errors, @new, @Default: nothing.

Returns:
  @has_errors prints `true` iff `@any` finding satisfies `f.level` equal to the @Error
    variant — an equality test, so @Warn and @Info never block; `false` on an empty
    collector.
  @to_text and @to_json return owned report @String per the Must framing entries (filter
    applied to finding blocks and array, footer and summary never).
  @to_junit returns the JUnit XML @String over the FULL set.
  The locators return a @BTreeMap ordered lexicographically by key, value = the key line's
    1-based number; empty map when the file cannot be read.
  @FromStr returns either the matching variant or the @String error above.

Raises:
  @to_junit @panic at exactly two documented sites (the doc `# Panics` header): an
    @write_xml failure yields `failed to write JUnit XML: {e}`; non-UTF-8 XML bytes yield
    `JUnit XML is not valid UTF-8: {e}`. Both are argued unreachable for in-memory finding
    data yet remain live panics.
  @to_json on a serde failure yields the empty @String via @unwrap_or_default — no error,
    an empty printed report (Tasks).
  Everything else is infallible: guarded IO only, zero @unwrap and zero @expect calls, no
    indexing, and line math through @saturating_add. The only non-panicking fallbacks are
    `@unwrap_or("unknown")` inside @to_junit (unreachable, Tasks) and @unwrap_or_default in
    @to_json.

Handles:
  `@line` of @None — text and JSON render the file with no `:` line suffix, JSON field
    carries `null`.
  Absolute finding files — @prefix_files leaves them untouched.
  Rules with no `/` — @to_junit files them under a suite named for the whole rule string.
  Missing or unreadable YAML in either locator — a silent empty map, so callers keep
    `@None` line findings.
  Duplicate keys inside a scanned section — the map's LATER line wins because @insert
    overwrites; the lexicographic map order stays unrelated to file order.
  Nested maps under a scanned key — lines deeper than the first observed key indent
    contribute no entry but still participate in the end-the-block indent test.

Must:
  # Severity ladder (the load-bearing order)
  The display gate is `f.level >= level_filter` on the derived @Ord — exact predicate:
    filter @Info lets `Info, Warn, Error` pass; filter @Warn lets `Warn, Error` pass and
    suppresses `Info`; filter @Error lets `Error` alone pass.
  The blocking predicate stays equality against @Error (@has_errors); the filter value never
    reaches it — display and verdict are independent lanes, matching the driver's
    "display-only `--level`" freeze.
  # Order and set semantics
  Zero dedup, zero sorting, zero mutation of levels anywhere in this file: @add and
    @extend append, @to_text and @to_json walk the @Vec in insertion order, so cross-rule
    and cross-checker interleaving follows the driver's call order (frozen there). The only
    re-map is @to_junit, which changes grouping but never within-suite content order.
  This file defines NO rule-code registry: the rule code families observed across the tree
    (`package/…`, `hbs/…`, `rhai/…`), their levels and enable/disable policy are producer
    call-site literals; @to_junit only splits the first `/`-segment.
  # Text framing (@to_text) — the rendering the driver delegates here
  Per DISPLAYED finding, three lines: `[`, the level @Display string at minimum width 5
    left-aligned, `] `, the rule, exactly two spaces, then `file:line` (bare `file` when
    @None), newline; then exactly eight spaces plus the raw message, newline; then an empty
    line. The tags therefore print as `[ERROR]`, `[WARN ]` and `[INFO ]`.
  The footer always prints, even over zero displayed findings: `Résultat : N erreurS,
    M warning` where S is `s` iff N is not 1, and the word `warning` never pluralizes; N
    and M count ALL stored findings (full set, filter ignored) and @Info appears nowhere
    in it.
  # JSON framing (@to_json) — the --format json wire names
  A pretty-printed object with exactly two top-level keys: `findings`, the array of the
    DISPLAYED findings in insertion order, each object carrying the @LintFinding fields in
    declaration order — `rule`, `level` (string `"Info"`, `"Warn"` or `"Error"`), `file`,
    `line` (positive integer or `null`), `message` — and `summary`, the object
    `{"errors": N, "warnings": M}` counted over the FULL set (same unfiltered semantics as
    the text footer; @Info uncounted).
  # JUnit rollup (@to_junit)
  No level filter and no footer: every stored finding, @Info and @Warn included, becomes a
    failure test case, and @file plus @line are dropped entirely.
  Grouping: the suite name is the rule text before the first `/` (the whole rule when no
    `/` exists), suites land in a @BTreeMap so they emit in lexicographic order, and cases
    within a suite keep insertion order; the case name is the full rule (duplicates
    allowed), @Duration::seconds(0) always, the failure type string is `lint`, and the
    failure content is @message.
  # Path rebasing (@prefix_files)
  A finding whose @file is relative per @is_relative is rewritten to `base.join(file)`;
    absolute ones pass through untouched; the rewrite is uniform over real files and the
    driver's virtual `package.yaml#…` identities, so it can create OS paths that never
    exist. A relative base keeps findings relative and a second call prepends again (not
    idempotent; the driver single-calls it per run path, Tasks).
  # Raw-YAML line locators (best-effort text scans, never parse YAML)
  @find_section_key_line_numbers opens at the FIRST line whose left-trimmed text is exactly
    `{section}:` or starts with `{section}: `; that header line's indent anchors the section.
    The first content line after the header fixes a single key indent, and entries are
    recorded ONLY from lines at exactly that indent — deeper (nested map) or shallower-yet-
    still-inside lines contribute no entry.
  The scanned block ends at the first content line whose indent is ≤ the header indent
    (loop break), so exactly one section instance per file is scanned; blank and `#` comment
    lines are skipped everywhere and never touch the indent bookkeeping.
  A key is the run before the first `:` left-trimmed (quotes preserved), empty keys are
    skipped, each kept key maps to its own 1-based line number via @saturating_add.
    @find_option_line_numbers is precisely this against section `options`; the in-crate
    consumers are the driver's @check_options and the unused-option report of
    ./hbs_checker.rs (missing map key there means a finding with a @None line).
  @config.rs seam frozen here: `override_` level values in `.vynil-lint.yaml` must be
    spelled `Info`, `Warn`, `Error`, because @LintLevel has no serde rename, so a lowercase
    level in the YAML rejects the WHOLE file back to defaults via the ./config.rs load lane
    — while @Display prints uppercase words and @FromStr accepts any case of the lowercase
    words: two separate case regimes on one field (Tasks).

Scenario: empty collector prints only the tally
  Given @LintResultCollector::new
  When @to_text runs with filter @Info
  Then the @String is the single tally line `Résultat : 0 erreur, 0 warning` and nothing
    else
  And @has_errors answers `false`
  Mapped test @collector_has_no_errors_when_empty pins only the @has_errors arm; the
    empty-set rendering has no test yet.

Scenario: any Error blocks, everything else passes
  Given a collector holding one finding at the @Error level added via @add
  Then @has_errors answers `true`
  But Given a warn-only, info-only or empty collector — @has_errors answers `false`
  Mapped test @collector_detects_error_level covers the `true` arm only.

Scenario: error filter hides lower findings but not the counts
  Given one @Error finding `test/error` and one @Warn finding `test/warn` on `test.txt`
  When @to_text runs with filter @Error
  Then blocks print for `test/error` only, in insertion order
  And the @String still counts 1 erreur and 1 warning in the footer despite @Warn being
    hidden
  Mapped test @to_text_filters_by_level asserts block presence and absence only; the
    footer-counts-the-full-set behaviour is untested.

Scenario: json report shows findings filtered and summary unfiltered
  Given the same @Error plus @Warn mix run through @to_json with filter @Error
  Then `findings` holds the one @Error object — fields `rule`, `"level": "Error"`, `file`,
    `"line": 1`, `message` — while `summary` reads `{"errors": 1, "warnings": 1}`
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: junit files every level as a failure under its category
  Given a `hbs/foo` @Error, a `rhai/bar` @Error and any lower-level finding
  When @to_junit runs
  Then suites `hbs` and `rhai` exist in lexicographic order with full-rule case names,
    failure type `lint`, @Duration::seconds(0) and no `file` or `line` markers anywhere
  But Given an @Info or @Warn finding — it appears identically as a failure case
  Mapped test @to_junit_groups_by_category asserts suite and case-name presence only.

Scenario: prefix_files rebases relative findings once
  Given findings on the relative path `sub/x.hbs`, on the virtual `package.yaml#value_script`
    and on one already-absolute path
  When @prefix_files runs with base `@path::Path::new("./pkg")`
  Then the two relative @file values become `./pkg/sub/x.hbs` and
    `./pkg/package.yaml#value_script`, still relative so a second call would prepend again,
    and the absolute one is unchanged
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: options line scan reads structure, never YAML semantics
  Given a file with `options:` at indent 2 and keys at indent 4, one nested map two deeper,
    a duplicate key later, comments and blank lines, plus a second `options:` section below
  When @find_option_line_numbers runs
  Then entries cover only the first block's indent-4 keys, the later duplicate wins its key,
    nested children and the second section are absent, comments and blanks stay skipped
  But Given an unreadable path — the map is empty, with no signal.
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  text block for a warn finding — note the level word padded to five inside the brackets:
    `[WARN ] package/unexpected-dir  pkg/vitals`
    `        Directory 'vitals' is not expected for System packages`
  then one empty line per finding block, and after any number of blocks the single tally:
    `Résultat : 2 erreurs, 1 warning`

Tasks:
  [ ] Add tests closing the unmapped behaviour frozen above: empty-set text rendering,
      footer and summary counting the full set under an applied filter, the json
      field-level wire shape (incl. `"line": null` and the capitalized `"level"`
      strings), @prefix_files relative / absolute / second-call behaviour, the locator
      indent, duplicate-key and unreadable matrix, @FromStr spelling rules and the
      @Display round-trip, and @to_junit level-independence.
  [?] @FromStr has no production caller anywhere in the workspace (config override maps go
      through serde, the driver keeps its own `@LevelFilter`): keep for a future `--level`
      env parse, or delete.
  [?] Case regimes: @serde against @LintLevel requires the spelled identifiers
      `Info`/`Warn`/`Error` — a lowercase `warn:` in `.vynil-lint.yaml` rejects the WHOLE
      config file back to defaults — while @Display and @FromStr speak the lowercase words.
      Decide whether @LintLevel gains a `rename_all = "lowercase"` serde attribute (wire
      change against ./config.rs expectations).
  [?] Footer and summary semantics: counts run over the full finding set while the bodies
      are filtered; `erreur` pluralizes but `warning` never does; @Info is counted nowhere.
      Freeze as-is or fix (CI parsing of tally and summary depends on it).
  [?] @to_junit turns EVERY finding into a JUnit failure regardless of @Info or @Warn, and
      drops @file and @line — acceptable for an all-or-nothing CI gate, or should levels
      map to distinct case kinds and locations be encoded?
  [?] `@unwrap_or("unknown")` inside @to_junit is unreachable (@str::split always yields at
      least one segment): dead branch to remove, and rules with no `/` silently form a
      full-rule-named suite — decide intended behaviour.
  [?] @find_section_key_line_numbers is generalized over `section` yet only ever called
      with `options`, opens the section at ANY indent depth (a nested `options:` matches by
      mistake) and lets duplicate keys keep the last line: keep the generic helper for a
      planned second section, or specialize it.
  [?] @to_json's @unwrap_or_default prints a silent empty @String on serde failure
      (practically impossible today — plain field types): acceptable as-is?

Done when:
  Every scenario above maps to a passing named test, each `[?]` decision is recorded (then
    honoured or amended in this spec), `cargo test` stays green, and `specdd lint` accepts
    this file.
